Z||000000||Richard Parnell||09-13-2000||10:58 AM||||Hello- In the middle of the V8 conversion right now so haven't been on the forum much lately.  Looking for a replacement AC compressor for the one I was using with my 4 cyl 87 SC.  They want well over $400.00 for a replacement here w/ trade in.  Anyone have a place I can get a better price.  Even with shipping to Hawaii, I should be able to get better price.  Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||09-13-2000||11:12 AM||||Richard, If you pay for travel, I'll personally hand carry a new compressor to Hawaii.<P>You may be able to locate one at Twin Lakes Fiero.. <A HREF="http://twinlakesfiero.tripod.com" TARGET=_blank>http://twinlakesfiero.tripod.com</A> <P>Bob may have one around.  When mine died he pulled one off the shelf, converted to the 134a and I was on my way.  He did a bunch of other stuff at the same time and the final bill was only $420.  Conversion to the 134A I think was $150.||||reg||10
Z||000002||theogre||09-13-2000||01:20 PM||||wrenchead.com or carparts.com||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||09-14-2000||03:21 PM||||Richard,<P>Why are you sticking with the old style of compressor? Now that you've "up" the horsepower and are going SBC why not upgrade the compresor to a Sandon or even a modern GM compresor off of another V8 car? There are all kinds of brackets available for your SBC that should gain clearance (factory and aftermarket). <P>Your evaporator and condensor could care less which compresor you use. All you have to do is install an inline high pressure switch and a wire to the electric clutch on the new compressor and have a couple of hoses re-made. Save yourself a few $$$ and stay away from a Fiero-only compressor.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-14-2000||11:11 PM||||neither type of compressor used in Fiero are "Fiero Only." The A6 was used in hundreds of thousands of GM cars as is the V5. There is nothing special about either for Fiero.<P>You already have the V5 compressor. I'd stay with it. It's much better than the old A6 that GM used. (V5 only came on 87-88 4 cylinder Fieros. All other Fieros had A6.)<P>There are versions of that compressor with both Seprpent and V belt clutches. You can even change the clutch if need be w/o loosing the refrigerant.<P>Depending on where you want to mount it. You should be able to find brackets for the V5 in most scrap yards. Note tho that you may need to get AC hoses made no matter what you do.||||reg||1
